<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikiza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1972 Burundian genocide</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ikiza ('the Catastrophe') was a campaign of mass killings in Burundi in 1972, in which the Tutsi-dominated army and the ruling party's youth wing killed perhaps 100,000 to 300,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>. It followed a Hutu rebellion in the south in April 1972 that had killed thousands of Tutsi. Educated Hutu were systematically targeted, and many scholars describe the killings as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikiza.jpg" alt="Ikiza" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, United States Central Intelligence Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CIA_map_of_Burundi_and_surrounding_countries_during_1972_killings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
